# plans/BUILD_AND_DEPENDENCY_SYSTEM_REDESIGN.md B13: `TestSuite`'s verbose /
# bail / parallel fields were carried by the evaluator's `BuildTestSuite` and
# forwarded by the runner, but `std/build.yo` never surfaced them — the builtin
# hard-coded `verbose : false, bail : false, parallel : 1`, so a build file
# could not ask for them at all.
#
# The fixture asks for all three, and two of them are observable here.
#
# `bail` stops the run at the second test, so the THIRD test's name must be
# ABSENT from the kept output and the total must be 2, not 3.
#
# `verbose` is NOT about naming the tests — `yo test` prints `✓ <name>` either
# way. It decides how much of a FAILING test's diagnostic is printed: the whole
# thing with `-v`, only its first line without (`src/main.yo`). So the assert's
# own message, `deliberate failure — the suite bails here`, is the line that
# disappears when `verbose` does not reach the builtin. `stdout_keep_match`
# keeps only the matched SUBSTRING, which drops the `(at file://…:NN:CC)` suffix
# that would otherwise move whenever std/assert.yo does.
#
# `parallel` cannot be observed here and is set anyway: it reaches the child as
# `--parallel 2`, which `yo test` v1 accepts and ignores (it runs files
# sequentially). Its presence at least keeps the extraction on a live path.
timeout=300
# Only the runner's own lines are compared: the child compile emits the host's
# ASan probe verdict, openssl paths and clang warnings with generated-C line
# numbers, none of which are this case's subject.
#
# The exit CODE is matched but not captured, deliberately. A failing `assert`
# aborts, and the number the runner prints for an abort is platform-specific:
# macOS reports the raw signal (6, SIGABRT) and Linux the shell encoding
# (128 + 6 = 134). Recording either one pins the case to the host that recorded
# it — this golden was recorded on macOS and could never pass on Linux, which
# is how it reached develop red. `stdout_keep_match` keeps only the matched
# SUBSTRING, so dropping `[0-9]+` from the pattern keeps the line and drops the
# number. The subject of this case is `bail` and `verbose`, not how an abort is
# encoded.
stdout_keep_match=(✓|✗) [a-z ]+|Test failed with exit code|deliberate failure — the suite bails here|Bailing out early due to test failure|[0-9]+ (passed|failed|total)
